.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMM-SET-LOG-LEVEL 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emm-set-log-level \- turn a module's own logging down or off
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli emm set-log-level
.BR \-\-module | \-m
.I MODULE
.BR \-\-level | \-l
.I LEVEL
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets the level a module's own output is logged at.
.PP
Everything a module process writes is read back by the manager and
logged on a channel of its own,
.IR module.<module> ,
verbatim. This is that channel's level, so
.B \-\-level off
silences the module in the manager's log entirely and
.B \-\-level error
keeps only what it wrote to standard error.
.PP
It takes effect on the manager's next reconcile \- within a few seconds
\- and restarts nothing. Unlike
.BR euclid-cli-emm-set-threads (1),
which can only be applied by cycling a module's instances because a
thread count is fixed when a process starts, a log level is not: every
instance keeps running and every request in flight is untouched.
.SH WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
This decides what the manager
.I passes on
, not what the module
.IR produces .
A line a module wrote to standard output arrives here as information
and one it wrote to standard error as an error, whatever the module's
own level said about it \- so turning a module
.I up
past
.I info
shows nothing new. What a module emits in the first place is
.I euclid.logging.level
(and
.IR euclid.logging.channels )
in its own process, re-read on
.BR SIGUSR1 :
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo \-e /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid.json
sudo pkill \-USR1 '^euclid\-'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Turning a module
.I down
or off, which is the reason this exists, works either way.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR
Module to set the level for, e.g. \fIesm\fR. Required. Module names are
lower case, and one given in capitals is read as the same module.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-level " " \fILEVEL\fR
One of \fIoff\fR, \fIerror\fR, \fIwarning\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIdebug\fR,
\fItrace\fR, or \fIdefault\fR to take the setting back and leave the
module under \fIeuclid.logging.channels\fR. Required. Anything else is
refused rather than interpreted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The level was recorded; the module, the level and the channel it
applies to are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
No module of that name exists, the level was not one, the server could
not be reached, or the caller is not an administrator.
.SH EXAMPLES
Silence a module that is drowning out everything else, then put it
back:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint https://localhost:5566 emm set-log-level \e
    --module esm --level off

euclid-cli --endpoint https://localhost:5566 emm set-log-level \e
    --module esm --level default
.fi
.RE
.PP
The level in force is reported by
.BR euclid-cli-emm-list-modules (1)
as
.IR logLevel .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-list-modules (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-set-threads (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-set-log-level (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jens Vogt <jens.vogt@opitz-consulting.com>
